<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Nightly backup of the database, the config and the plist.

  StartCalendarInterval rather than StartInterval: launchd runs a missed
  calendar job once the machine wakes, so a mini that was asleep at 03:15 still
  gets its backup. A repeating interval would simply have skipped the night.

  The placeholders below are substituted at install time rather than hand-edited.
  Do not copy this file into /Library/LaunchDaemons unchanged: the account it
  names does not exist, and launchd fails such a job before it opens a log, so
  the only symptom is silence. See "Install the launchd jobs" in DEPLOY.md, which
  does both plists in one paste:

    sudo launchctl print system/com.building-controls.backup | grep username
      -> must show your account. Anything else means it was installed unedited.

  Run it now, without waiting for 03:15:
    sudo launchctl kickstart -k system/com.building-controls.backup

  Check it ran:
    tail /usr/local/var/log/building-controls-backup.log
    ls -lt /usr/local/var/backups/building-controls | head

  An EMPTY OR ABSENT LOG IS NOT "IT RAN AND SAID NOTHING" -- this script always
  prints. It means no process ever started. Read `launchctl print`:

    last exit code = 78: EX_CONFIG   the UserName does not exist. launchd gave up
                                     at user lookup, before opening the log, which
                                     is why there is no log rather than a log
                                     containing an error.
    state = spawn scheduled          plus "active count = 0" and no log: launchd
    with no exit code                could not open StandardOutPath as UserName.
    Could not find service           never bootstrapped. Copying a plist does not
                                     install it, and kickstart on a service that
                                     does not exist creates nothing.

  "properties = penalty box" means launchd has throttled it after repeated
  failures. bootout then bootstrap clears it; kickstart alone does not.

  launchd has no shell, no PATH and no home directory, so every path here must be
  absolute.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>com.building-controls.backup</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/CHANGEME/building-controls/deploy/backup.sh</string>
    </array>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/CHANGEME/building-controls</string>

    <!--
      Same account as the gateway. Running the backup as root would write files
      the ordinary account cannot read, and a backup you need sudo to look at is
      one you will not check.
    -->
    <key>UserName</key>
    <string>CHANGEME</string>

    <key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
    <dict>
        <key>Hour</key>
        <integer>3</integer>
        <key>Minute</key>
        <integer>15</integer>
    </dict>

    <!-- A backup job must not be restarted forever if it fails. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <false/>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <false/>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/usr/local/var/log/building-controls-backup.log</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/usr/local/var/log/building-controls-backup.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
